#{[17592186522711 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522711 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66856" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522711 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522711 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{e})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522711 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f25e707-eeb9-5b06-994f-863f6b76503d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522711 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\sqrt[3]{e}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{e}={e}^{1/3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{e})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/3})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/3})=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522721 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522721 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522721 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522721 #edb/ref 1071 "$2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522721 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ea37247-24cf-5fab-829f-0e5fcfa54afc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522721 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}={e}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}})=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-4})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-4})=2\\cdot (-4)=-8\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522766 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522766 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522766 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522766 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{e\\,}≈1.396$ can be expressed as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522766 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4f69e59-b897-52a3-98ba-7aff5af1aea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522766 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the given approximation can be written using exponents as:\n\n$$\n{e}^{1/3}≈1.396\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={e}^{x}$ is equivalent to $\\operatorname{ln}⁡a=x$ \n\nIf we compare ${e}^{1/3}≈1.396$ with $a={e}^{x},$ we get $a=1.396$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the approximation $\\sqrt[3]{e}≈1.396$ can be expressed as $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.396)≈\\frac{1}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522777 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522777 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522777 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522777 #edb/ref 1071 "$-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522777 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc51ccb0-0438-5418-a3d9-157e172ac1ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522777 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}={e}^{-5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}})=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-5})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-5.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-5})=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-5)=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
